{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011M6LGziZQtx1AzmkibXuQx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Changelog\n2\t\n3\tAll notable changes to **ceo-orchestration** are documented in this file.\n4\t\n5\tThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\n6\tand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n7\t\n8\t> **Scope.** This log records *user-visible* changes — new skills, hooks, slash\n9\t> commands, schema/contract changes, and behavior an adopter would notice after\n10\t> installing or upgrading the framework. Internal refactors, test-only churn, and\n11\t> release-engineering bookkeeping are omitted. Counts cited below (as of\n12\t> v1.4.4: 166 skills, 27 slash commands, 201 ADRs, 78 `_lib` modules) are\n13\t> reproducible from the repository via\n14\t> `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## [1.4.4] - 2026-11-03\n19\t\n20\tMaintenance release (PLAN-198). **v1.4.3 never reached npm.** Only\n21\t`v1.4.3-rc.1` was tagged (2026-10-17) and published on GitHub as a\n22\tpre-release. The npm workflow does not publish an rc tag, and no v1.4.3 GA\n23\twas cut, so npm `latest` was still 1.4.2 when this entry was written\n24\t(2026-11-03). The `[1.4.3]` entry below stays as written: it describes the\n25\trc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\n26\tin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\n27\tComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n28\t\n29\tThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\n30\tPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n31\t`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\n32\tthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\n33\tWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n34\t\n35\t- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n36\t  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n37\t- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n38\t  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n39\t  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n40\t- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n41\t  cure).\n42\t- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n43\t  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n44\t  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n45\t- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n46\t  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n47\t  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n48\t  below).\n49\t- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n50\t  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n51\t  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n52\t- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n53\t  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n54\t  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n55\t  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n56\t\n57\tThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\n58\tthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\n59\tchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\n60\tmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO\n61\tfloor, the model allowlist and the Codex pin. All of this is stated below\n62\tand declared in Known-open, with no release assigned to its cure. Two of\n63\tthe defects this release fixes: the preparation of this release found\n64\tthat the kernel's hard-deny list had not followed the code that moved out\n65\tof seven kernel entry points; and the secret redactor, since v1.0.0, cut\n66\tthe text at offsets taken from its normalized form, which the signed\n67\tv1.4.0 verdict had declared as its condition 58. One condition of the redactor\n68\tfix is declared in Known-open. The spool cure is partial, and the new\n69\tguard's false-positive limits were never measured on this implementation.\n70\tNo speed claim.\n71\t\n72\t**Version number.** This release keeps a PATCH number, 1.4.4, by the\n73\tOwner's decision. `VERSIONING.md` classes an additive SPEC change (the two\n74\taudit-log enums below) and a new trust boundary as MINOR, and this train\n75\thas both: the indirect-execution guard on by default, and the model checks\n76\tand Codex pin above. Plan an upgrade from v1.4.2 as you would plan a MINOR\n77\tone.\n78\t\n79\t### Added — a Bash guard against indirect execution, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201)\n80\t\n81\t- After its existing checks, `check_bash_safety.py` runs one bounded scan\n82\t  (`_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py`). The scan looks for `rm -rf`,\n83\t  `git reset --hard` or a forced `git push` reached indirectly:\n84\t  - inside `sh -c`, `eval` or a here-doc;\n85\t  - behind a known launcher;\n86\t  - through a computed command name;\n87\t  - in a program fed by a pipe, a redirect, a descriptor or a process\n88\t    substitution.\n89\t- It also blocks a computed write when it resolves the target from the\n90\t  command text to a canonical path.\n91\t- It only adds blocks; an earlier BLOCK stays BLOCK. Its reason codes are\n92\t  `bash_indirect_*` and `bash_computed_canonical_target`.\n93\t- It fails closed on a recognized shell body it cannot parse, and at its\n94\t  depth, size and word limits.\n95\t- Two switches control it. Both are on unless set to `0` in the\n96\t  environment Claude Code starts with:\n97\t  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0` turns off the scan;\n98\t  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0` turns off only the computed-verb and\n99\t    opaque-feeder forms.\n100\t- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n101\t  session and switch.\n102\t\n103\t### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n104\t\n105\t- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n106\t  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n107\t  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n108\t- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n109\t\n110\t### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n111\t\n112\t- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n113\t  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n114\t  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and\n115\t  `check_bash_safety.py` becomes a 42-line entry point. Among the scripts,\n116\t  `audit-query.py`, `check_contamination.py` and\n117\t  `check-installer-write-safety.py` move the same way.\n118\t- Installs and upgrades receive the packages: the installer copies every\n119\t  `_lib` entry except the test harness. On the merge of pull request #7,\n120\t  the Smoke Install push run passed; a manually dispatched run was\n121\t  cancelled.\n122\t- If you patched a hook by hand, the entry point no longer holds the logic.\n123\t  Re-apply the patch to the module.\n124\t- The compatibility evidence is the test suites. No differential replay\n125\t  against the code before the split is claimed.\n126\t\n127\t### Changed — a registered hook leaves its spool work pending at exit (ADR-055-AMEND-4, partial)\n128\t\n129\t- `_python-hook.sh` exports `CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1`. A hook that runs\n130\t  with it does not drain its audit spool at exit or on a signal. It leaves\n131\t  an `exit_deadline_skip` breadcrumb instead, and its events wait in the\n132\t  spool for a later drain, so they can reach the log later.\n133\t- This removes the forced exit drain that could outlast a hook's timeout.\n134\t- ADR-055-AMEND-4 is `ACCEPTED` with its implementation `PARTIAL`\n135\t  (Known-open).\n136\t\n137\t### Security and governance — what changed without the signing ceremony\n138\t\n139\t- **How it arrived.** Pull requests #6 and #7 brought 233 canonical paths\n140\t  into `main` with no sentinel and no signature file. Fifteen of them are\n141\t  kernel paths by the kernel list as merged: among them the Bash-guard,\n142\t  canonical-edit, pair-rail, policy and audit-writer hooks, the\n143\t  agent-frontmatter VETO validator, the Codex pin file, the framework's own\n144\t  settings and `validate.yml`. By the list this release restores (Fixed,\n145\t  below), 101 of them are. The merge commit of #7 carries a GPG signature,\n146\t  which is not the signing ceremony. No signed sentinel ratifies those\n147\t  files as of this entry, and nothing in the repository requires one:\n148\t  `main` has no branch protection and no ruleset (measured through the\n149\t  GitHub API on 2026-11-03).\n150\t- **The VETO floor moved from a closed list to family patterns.**\n151\t  - Before, an agent file with `veto_floor: true` had to declare one of\n152\t    four Owner-signed Claude ids.\n153\t  - Now `agent_frontmatter.py` and `escalation_signals.py` also accept any\n154\t    id that matches the families in `_lib/model_currency.py`: Claude Opus\n155\t    majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9` and `o3` to `o9`, with numeric and\n156\t    lowercase-word suffixes.\n157\t  - The predicate refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`. It admits ids\n158\t    that the deprecation ledger (`.claude/scripts/model-deprecations.json`)\n159\t    lists as retired or retiring, among them dated Claude Opus 4 and 4.1\n160\t    ids, `gpt-5`, `gpt-5-mini`, `o3`, `o3-mini` and `o4-mini` (probed on\n161\t    2026-11-03).\n162\t  - The docstring of `agent_frontmatter.py` still describes the closed\n163\t    list.\n164\t- **The model allowlist moved to wider family patterns.**\n165\t  - Before, the settings-tamper detector, the agent `model:` check of\n166\t    `validate-governance.sh` and the tier-policy loader accepted only an\n167\t    exact member of a closed list.\n168\t  - Now all three call `is_allowed_model` in `_lib/model_currency.py`\n169\t    (ADR-149 Amendment 4). Besides the listed ids, it admits Claude Opus,\n170\t    Sonnet and Haiku majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9`, `o3` to `o9`,\n171\t    `gemini-2` to `gemini-9` and `grok-3` to `grok-9`, with numeric and\n172\t    lowercase-word suffixes.\n173\t  - It refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`, so ids that the\n174\t    deprecation ledger lists as retired pass, among them\n175\t    `claude-sonnet-4-[REDACTED:rg]` and `claude-opus-4-1-[REDACTED:rg]` (probed on\n176\t    2026-11-03).\n177\t  - So `settings_tamper_model_remap`, which `/ceo-boot` reports, no longer\n178\t    flags a model variable (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`\n179\t    or an `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*` key) set to such an id, for example\n180\t    `gemini-2.5-pro` or `grok-4`, in the environment or in a settings\n181\t    `env` block. An agent file with such a `model:` passes\n182\t    `validate-governance.sh`.\n183\t  - The shipped `availableModels` is unchanged.\n184\t- **The Codex pin moved from an exact payload hash to a version floor.**\n185\t  - Before, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` failed closed on any\n186\t    Codex binary whose payload sha256 differed from the signed manifest,\n187\t    which pins one binary of Codex CLI 0.160.0.\n188\t  - Now a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version passes as\n189\t    `compatible/version_floor_only` when a nearby `@openai/codex`\n190\t    `package.json` declares a stable version at or above 0.160.0\n191\t    (`_lib/cli_currency.py`). The payload's sha256 is still computed and\n192\t    recorded, but on this route a mismatch with the manifest no longer\n193\t    fails.\n194\t  - The release gate follows: `validate-pair-rail-verdict.py` only checks\n195\t    the format of the payload sha of a verdict that declares such a\n196\t    version. Its range check reads the pin file; its payload-hash\n197\t    exemption uses the code constant 0.160.0\n198\t    (`cli_currency.CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR`).\n199\t  - The ADR-182 amendment states the floor as `>=0.160.0`. The pin file\n200\t    reads `>=0.128.0`, with no upper bound: a CI fix in the same pull\n201\t    request lowered it after the amendment.\n202\t  - The operating contract still says the reviewer is pinned by hash, not\n203\t    by range. That no longer describes `main`.\n204\t  - Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`, so there the pin still\n205\t    resolves as infrastructure and fails open (advisory). That is\n206\t    unchanged.\n207\t  - With `--harness codex`, the installer now prints that it was verified\n208\t    against Codex CLI 0.160.0, while the hook fixtures date from 0.139.0.\n209\t\n210\t### Fixed — the kernel's hard-deny list covers the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the live-adapter package\n211\t\n212\t- The split (PLAN-197) moved the code of seven kernel entry points into\n213\t  helper packages: five hooks (canonical edit, pair rail, agent spawn,\n214\t  audit log and Bash safety) and two hook library modules (policy and\n215\t  audit emit). The same train replaced the plan-path regexes of two\n216\t  kernel files, the plan-lifecycle hook (`check_plan_edit.py`) and the\n217\t  policy preprocessors (`_lib/policy_preprocessors.py`), with the\n218\t  plan-path predicate in `_lib/plan_paths.py`. The kernel's hard-deny\n219\t  list (`check_arbitration_kernel.py`) kept naming only the kernel files,\n220\t  so the 81 files of those packages and the predicate were canonical\n221\t  only: a signed sentinel could unlock them. The list now has a row for\n222\t  each, so that code is hard-deny again, as it was inside the kernel\n223\t  files in v1.4.2.\n224\t- The list also enrolls four modules that kernel code calls, which were\n225\t  canonical only as well: `_lib/model_currency.py`, the VETO-floor\n226\t  predicate that the kernel files `agent_frontmatter.py` and\n227\t  `escalation_signals.py` now call as part of their VETO-floor check; and\n228\t  three modules this train added, `_lib/cli_currency.py` (the Codex\n229\t  payload-pin currency check, called by the pair-rail code),\n230\t  `_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py` (the indirect-execution matcher) and\n231\t  `_lib/bash_indirect_telemetry.py` (its disabled-rail telemetry), both\n232\t  called by the Bash guard's code.\n233\t- The live-adapter package (`_lib/adapters/live/`, 11 files: the ADR-040\n234\t  provider adapters, off by default), which the kernel\n235\t  `_lib/adapters/__init__.py` loads, was canonical only as well, since\n236\t  v1.0.0. It is now enrolled in the kernel, so an edit to it goes through\n237\t  the kernel override like an edit to any kernel file.\n238\t- The list now has one recursive row per package (the seven helper\n239\t  packages and `adapters/live/`), so a file added there later is a kernel\n240\t  path too, and one row for each of the five flat modules: 13 new rows,\n241\t  and 125 tracked kernel files become 222.\n242\t- A new test (`tests/test_kernel_decomposition_coverage.py`) derives from\n243\t  the code each helper package below `_lib/` that a kernel source reaches\n244\t  by an import statement or by a `/` chain of string literals, and checks\n245\t  that each one is kernel in full, with a positive control on the known\n246\t  packages. It also checks that each of the five flat modules is kernel\n247\t  and still imported by a kernel source. Dropping any one of the 13 new\n248\t  rows turns it red.\n249\t- No tag shipped the gap this train opened: none of the seven packages\n250\t  and five flat modules existed at v1.4.2 or v1.4.3-rc.1.\n251\t- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by two Claude\n252\t  refuters and Codex. No sentinel file covers it.\n253\t\n254\t### Fixed — the secret redactor's offsets after an NFKC-changing character (present since v1.0.0)\n255\t\n256\t- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n257\t  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n258\t  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n259\t  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n260\t  shifted the cut, so part or all of the secret could survive next to its\n261\t  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n262\t  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n263\t  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its condition 58.\n264\t- `scan()` now reports offsets in the text passed in. It matches that text\n265\t  and its NFKC view, maps the view's matches back, and merges overlapping\n266\t  findings into one span. The output is the original text with only those\n267\t  spans replaced; no normalized character reaches it.\n268\t- The pattern catalog is unchanged.\n269\t- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by Codex and two\n270\t  Claude refuters over several rounds.\n271\t  Codex approved it with one condition, which predates the fix and is\n272\t  declared in Known-open. No sentinel file covers it.\n273\t\n274\t### Fixed — backup and restore\n275\t\n276\t- `ceo-backup.sh` archives only the regular files of `memory/`; a link\n277\t  there is left out.\n278\t- `ceo-restore.sh`:\n279\t  - checks every archive member before extraction (`_restore_archive.py`):\n280\t    no links, no absolute paths, no `..`, no name outside the layout, and\n281\t    never `audit-key` or `.salt`, in any letter case;\n282\t  - resolves `--dest` physically and refuses a link, or a file, as the\n283\t    destination;\n284\t  - refuses a restore target that is a link or sits under one;\n285\t  - runs the same checks in the preview and in the apply.\n286\t- On macOS, the system `tar` adds a hidden AppleDouble member\n287\t  (`._<name>`) for each file or folder with extended attributes, and\n288\t  `ceo-restore.sh` refused every such archive (`archive refused path:\n289\t  ._.`). `ceo-backup.sh` no longer writes these members. For archives\n290\t  that already have them, restore skips such a member, without writing\n291\t  it, only when its matching file or folder is in the same archive; any\n292\t  other `._` member is refused. Restore now extracts only the members it\n293\t  accepted, in Python instead of with `tar -x`: a truncated or corrupt\n294\t  archive is refused before anything is written, and so is an archive\n295\t  with a sparse member or with two members of the same name.\n296\t- The disaster-recovery and incident-response guides no longer pass\n297\t  `--dry-run`, a flag the script refuses. The preview is the default, and\n298\t  `--apply` restores.\n299\t\n300\t### Fixed — `audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter`\n301\t\n302\t- With the flag, every result maps to a documented exit code with a named\n303\t  reason, one JSON object under `--json`, and no traceback.\n304\t- An absent counter exits 0 and reports `chain_length_source: \"absent\"`.\n305\t  An unreadable counter exits 3.\n306\t- A file and stdin go through one strict decode, and a log that is not a\n307\t  regular file is refused.\n308\t- Without the flag, nothing changes.\n309\t\n310\t### Fixed — three findings of the v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass\n311\t\n312\t- `diagnose-output-hook.py` can no longer write to the live audit log.\n313\t- `check-audit-real-loss.py` reports a malformed `record_id` instead of\n314\t  crashing.\n315\t- `audit_spool_state.py` and `check-audit-real-loss.py`, both read-only,\n316\t  stop writing bytecode.\n317\t\n318\t### Fixed — harness gate, root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion, plan lifecycle, cost envelope\n319\t\n320\t- `check-active-hooks-executable.py` validates a hook's `type` and\n321\t  `matcher` the way the loader does. A settings path that exists but is\n322\t  not a regular file, and a filesystem error while resolving a hook path,\n323\t  exit 2 with a named error.\n324\t- `check_harness_config.py` turns an exception in that sub-check into a\n325\t  RED finding and keeps its output. Before, it was a degraded warning,\n326\t  which reads as green.\n327\t- A `claudeMdExcludes` value that excludes the root `CLAUDE.md` is\n328\t  classified as settings tamper (`settings_tamper_claude_md_excludes_root`).\n329\t- The harness gate adds an advisory finding, `root_instruction_load`. It\n330\t  reports `root_load_unobservable` unless you pass a complete list of the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"046a4659-3b58-4aa1-afb8-7e9a6b625253","uuid":"a5fe91bc-1d27-47db-8ecd-b8a577ab6d78","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:04:19.289Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to **ceo-orchestration** are documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n\n> **Scope.** This log records *user-visible* changes — new skills, hooks, slash\n> commands, schema/contract changes, and behavior an adopter would notice after\n> installing or upgrading the framework. Internal refactors, test-only churn, and\n> release-engineering bookkeeping are omitted. Counts cited below (as of\n> v1.4.4: 166 skills, 27 slash commands, 201 ADRs, 78 `_lib` modules) are\n> reproducible from the repository via\n> `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`.\n\n---\n\n## [1.4.4] - 2026-11-03\n\nMaintenance release (PLAN-198). **v1.4.3 never reached npm.** Only\n`v1.4.3-rc.1` was tagged (2026-10-17) and published on GitHub as a\npre-release. The npm workflow does not publish an rc tag, and no v1.4.3 GA\nwas cut, so npm `latest` was still 1.4.2 when this entry was written\n(2026-11-03). The `[1.4.3]` entry below stays as written: it describes the\nrc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\nin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\nComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n\nThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\nPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\nthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\nWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n\n- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n  cure).\n- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n  below).\n- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n\nThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\nthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\nchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\nmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO\nfloor, the model allowlist and the Codex pin. All of this is stated below\nand declared in Known-open, with no release assigned to its cure. Two of\nthe defects this release fixes: the preparation of this release found\nthat the kernel's hard-deny list had not followed the code that moved out\nof seven kernel entry points; and the secret redactor, since v1.0.0, cut\nthe text at offsets taken from its normalized form, which the signed\nv1.4.0 verdict had declared as its condition 58. One condition of the redactor\nfix is declared in Known-open. The spool cure is partial, and the new\nguard's false-positive limits were never measured on this implementation.\nNo speed claim.\n\n**Version number.** This release keeps a PATCH number, 1.4.4, by the\nOwner's decision. `VERSIONING.md` classes an additive SPEC change (the two\naudit-log enums below) and a new trust boundary as MINOR, and this train\nhas both: the indirect-execution guard on by default, and the model checks\nand Codex pin above. Plan an upgrade from v1.4.2 as you would plan a MINOR\none.\n\n### Added — a Bash guard against indirect execution, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201)\n\n- After its existing checks, `check_bash_safety.py` runs one bounded scan\n  (`_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py`). The scan looks for `rm -rf`,\n  `git reset --hard` or a forced `git push` reached indirectly:\n  - inside `sh -c`, `eval` or a here-doc;\n  - behind a known launcher;\n  - through a computed command name;\n  - in a program fed by a pipe, a redirect, a descriptor or a process\n    substitution.\n- It also blocks a computed write when it resolves the target from the\n  command text to a canonical path.\n- It only adds blocks; an earlier BLOCK stays BLOCK. Its reason codes are\n  `bash_indirect_*` and `bash_computed_canonical_target`.\n- It fails closed on a recognized shell body it cannot parse, and at its\n  depth, size and word limits.\n- Two switches control it. Both are on unless set to `0` in the\n  environment Claude Code starts with:\n  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0` turns off the scan;\n  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0` turns off only the computed-verb and\n    opaque-feeder forms.\n- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n  session and switch.\n\n### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n\n- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n\n### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n\n- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and\n  `check_bash_safety.py` becomes a 42-line entry point. Among the scripts,\n  `audit-query.py`, `check_contamination.py` and\n  `check-installer-write-safety.py` move the same way.\n- Installs and upgrades receive the packages: the installer copies every\n  `_lib` entry except the test harness. On the merge of pull request #7,\n  the Smoke Install push run passed; a manually dispatched run was\n  cancelled.\n- If you patched a hook by hand, the entry point no longer holds the logic.\n  Re-apply the patch to the module.\n- The compatibility evidence is the test suites. No differential replay\n  against the code before the split is claimed.\n\n### Changed — a registered hook leaves its spool work pending at exit (ADR-055-AMEND-4, partial)\n\n- `_python-hook.sh` exports `CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1`. A hook that runs\n  with it does not drain its audit spool at exit or on a signal. It leaves\n  an `exit_deadline_skip` breadcrumb instead, and its events wait in the\n  spool for a later drain, so they can reach the log later.\n- This removes the forced exit drain that could outlast a hook's timeout.\n- ADR-055-AMEND-4 is `ACCEPTED` with its implementation `PARTIAL`\n  (Known-open).\n\n### Security and governance — what changed without the signing ceremony\n\n- **How it arrived.** Pull requests #6 and #7 brought 233 canonical paths\n  into `main` with no sentinel and no signature file. Fifteen of them are\n  kernel paths by the kernel list as merged: among them the Bash-guard,\n  canonical-edit, pair-rail, policy and audit-writer hooks, the\n  agent-frontmatter VETO validator, the Codex pin file, the framework's own\n  settings and `validate.yml`. By the list this release restores (Fixed,\n  below), 101 of them are. The merge commit of #7 carries a GPG signature,\n  which is not the signing ceremony. No signed sentinel ratifies those\n  files as of this entry, and nothing in the repository requires one:\n  `main` has no branch protection and no ruleset (measured through the\n  GitHub API on 2026-11-03).\n- **The VETO floor moved from a closed list to family patterns.**\n  - Before, an agent file with `veto_floor: true` had to declare one of\n    four Owner-signed Claude ids.\n  - Now `agent_frontmatter.py` and `escalation_signals.py` also accept any\n    id that matches the families in `_lib/model_currency.py`: Claude Opus\n    majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9` and `o3` to `o9`, with numeric and\n    lowercase-word suffixes.\n  - The predicate refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`. It admits ids\n    that the deprecation ledger (`.claude/scripts/model-deprecations.json`)\n    lists as retired or retiring, among them dated Claude Opus 4 and 4.1\n    ids, `gpt-5`, `gpt-5-mini`, `o3`, `o3-mini` and `o4-mini` (probed on\n    2026-11-03).\n  - The docstring of `agent_frontmatter.py` still describes the closed\n    list.\n- **The model allowlist moved to wider family patterns.**\n  - Before, the settings-tamper detector, the agent `model:` check of\n    `validate-governance.sh` and the tier-policy loader accepted only an\n    exact member of a closed list.\n  - Now all three call `is_allowed_model` in `_lib/model_currency.py`\n    (ADR-149 Amendment 4). Besides the listed ids, it admits Claude Opus,\n    Sonnet and Haiku majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9`, `o3` to `o9`,\n    `gemini-2` to `gemini-9` and `grok-3` to `grok-9`, with numeric and\n    lowercase-word suffixes.\n  - It refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`, so ids that the\n    deprecation ledger lists as retired pass, among them\n    `claude-sonnet-4-[REDACTED:rg]` and `claude-opus-4-1-[REDACTED:rg]` (probed on\n    2026-11-03).\n  - So `settings_tamper_model_remap`, which `/ceo-boot` reports, no longer\n    flags a model variable (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`\n    or an `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*` key) set to such an id, for example\n    `gemini-2.5-pro` or `grok-4`, in the environment or in a settings\n    `env` block. An agent file with such a `model:` passes\n    `validate-governance.sh`.\n  - The shipped `availableModels` is unchanged.\n- **The Codex pin moved from an exact payload hash to a version floor.**\n  - Before, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` failed closed on any\n    Codex binary whose payload sha256 differed from the signed manifest,\n    which pins one binary of Codex CLI 0.160.0.\n  - Now a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version passes as\n    `compatible/version_floor_only` when a nearby `@openai/codex`\n    `package.json` declares a stable version at or above 0.160.0\n    (`_lib/cli_currency.py`). The payload's sha256 is still computed and\n    recorded, but on this route a mismatch with the manifest no longer\n    fails.\n  - The release gate follows: `validate-pair-rail-verdict.py` only checks\n    the format of the payload sha of a verdict that declares such a\n    version. Its range check reads the pin file; its payload-hash\n    exemption uses the code constant 0.160.0\n    (`cli_currency.CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR`).\n  - The ADR-182 amendment states the floor as `>=0.160.0`. The pin file\n    reads `>=0.128.0`, with no upper bound: a CI fix in the same pull\n    request lowered it after the amendment.\n  - The operating contract still says the reviewer is pinned by hash, not\n    by range. That no longer describes `main`.\n  - Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`, so there the pin still\n    resolves as infrastructure and fails open (advisory). That is\n    unchanged.\n  - With `--harness codex`, the installer now prints that it was verified\n    against Codex CLI 0.160.0, while the hook fixtures date from 0.139.0.\n\n### Fixed — the kernel's hard-deny list covers the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the live-adapter package\n\n- The split (PLAN-197) moved the code of seven kernel entry points into\n  helper packages: five hooks (canonical edit, pair rail, agent spawn,\n  audit log and Bash safety) and two hook library modules (policy and\n  audit emit). The same train replaced the plan-path regexes of two\n  kernel files, the plan-lifecycle hook (`check_plan_edit.py`) and the\n  policy preprocessors (`_lib/policy_preprocessors.py`), with the\n  plan-path predicate in `_lib/plan_paths.py`. The kernel's hard-deny\n  list (`check_arbitration_kernel.py`) kept naming only the kernel files,\n  so the 81 files of those packages and the predicate were canonical\n  only: a signed sentinel could unlock them. The list now has a row for\n  each, so that code is hard-deny again, as it was inside the kernel\n  files in v1.4.2.\n- The list also enrolls four modules that kernel code calls, which were\n  canonical only as well: `_lib/model_currency.py`, the VETO-floor\n  predicate that the kernel files `agent_frontmatter.py` and\n  `escalation_signals.py` now call as part of their VETO-floor check; and\n  three modules this train added, `_lib/cli_currency.py` (the Codex\n  payload-pin currency check, called by the pair-rail code),\n  `_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py` (the indirect-execution matcher) and\n  `_lib/bash_indirect_telemetry.py` (its disabled-rail telemetry), both\n  called by the Bash guard's code.\n- The live-adapter package (`_lib/adapters/live/`, 11 files: the ADR-040\n  provider adapters, off by default), which the kernel\n  `_lib/adapters/__init__.py` loads, was canonical only as well, since\n  v1.0.0. It is now enrolled in the kernel, so an edit to it goes through\n  the kernel override like an edit to any kernel file.\n- The list now has one recursive row per package (the seven helper\n  packages and `adapters/live/`), so a file added there later is a kernel\n  path too, and one row for each of the five flat modules: 13 new rows,\n  and 125 tracked kernel files become 222.\n- A new test (`tests/test_kernel_decomposition_coverage.py`) derives from\n  the code each helper package below `_lib/` that a kernel source reaches\n  by an import statement or by a `/` chain of string literals, and checks\n  that each one is kernel in full, with a positive control on the known\n  packages. It also checks that each of the five flat modules is kernel\n  and still imported by a kernel source. Dropping any one of the 13 new\n  rows turns it red.\n- No tag shipped the gap this train opened: none of the seven packages\n  and five flat modules existed at v1.4.2 or v1.4.3-rc.1.\n- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by two Claude\n  refuters and Codex. No sentinel file covers it.\n\n### Fixed — the secret redactor's offsets after an NFKC-changing character (present since v1.0.0)\n\n- `scan()` in `_lib/secret_patterns.py` matched the NFKC-normalized view\n  of a text but reported offsets in that view, while `redact()` and\n  `scan_and_redact()` cut the original text with them. A character whose\n  NFKC form has another length (an ellipsis, a ligature) before a secret\n  shifted the cut, so part or all of the secret could survive next to its\n  label. The Codex and Grok egress redactor (`_lib/codex_egress_redact.py`)\n  uses these functions. Every release since v1.0.0 has the defect. The\n  signed v1.4.0 verdict declared this defect as its condition 58.\n- `scan()` now reports offsets in the text passed in. It matches that text\n  and its NFKC view, maps the view's matches back, and merges overlapping\n  findings into one span. The output is the original text with only those\n  spans replaced; no normalized character reaches it.\n- The pattern catalog is unchanged.\n- The fix is a commit the maintainer signed, after review by Codex and two\n  Claude refuters over several rounds.\n  Codex approved it with one condition, which predates the fix and is\n  declared in Known-open. No sentinel file covers it.\n\n### Fixed — backup and restore\n\n- `ceo-backup.sh` archives only the regular files of `memory/`; a link\n  there is left out.\n- `ceo-restore.sh`:\n  - checks every archive member before extraction (`_restore_archive.py`):\n    no links, no absolute paths, no `..`, no name outside the layout, and\n    never `audit-key` or `.salt`, in any letter case;\n  - resolves `--dest` physically and refuses a link, or a file, as the\n    destination;\n  - refuses a restore target that is a link or sits under one;\n  - runs the same checks in the preview and in the apply.\n- On macOS, the system `tar` adds a hidden AppleDouble member\n  (`._<name>`) for each file or folder with extended attributes, and\n  `ceo-restore.sh` refused every such archive (`archive refused path:\n  ._.`). `ceo-backup.sh` no longer writes these members. For archives\n  that already have them, restore skips such a member, without writing\n  it, only when its matching file or folder is in the same archive; any\n  other `._` member is refused. Restore now extracts only the members it\n  accepted, in Python instead of with `tar -x`: a truncated or corrupt\n  archive is refused before anything is written, and so is an archive\n  with a sparse member or with two members of the same name.\n- The disaster-recovery and incident-response guides no longer pass\n  `--dry-run`, a flag the script refuses. The preview is the default, and\n  `--apply` restores.\n\n### Fixed — `audit-verify-chain.py --strict-against-counter`\n\n- With the flag, every result maps to a documented exit code with a named\n  reason, one JSON object under `--json`, and no traceback.\n- An absent counter exits 0 and reports `chain_length_source: \"absent\"`.\n  An unreadable counter exits 3.\n- A file and stdin go through one strict decode, and a log that is not a\n  regular file is refused.\n- Without the flag, nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed — three findings of the v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass\n\n- `diagnose-output-hook.py` can no longer write to the live audit log.\n- `check-audit-real-loss.py` reports a malformed `record_id` instead of\n  crashing.\n- `audit_spool_state.py` and `check-audit-real-loss.py`, both read-only,\n  stop writing bytecode.\n\n### Fixed — harness gate, root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion, plan lifecycle, cost envelope\n\n- `check-active-hooks-executable.py` validates a hook's `type` and\n  `matcher` the way the loader does. A settings path that exists but is\n  not a regular file, and a filesystem error while resolving a hook path,\n  exit 2 with a named error.\n- `check_harness_config.py` turns an exception in that sub-check into a\n  RED finding and keeps its output. Before, it was a degraded warning,\n  which reads as green.\n- A `claudeMdExcludes` value that excludes the root `CLAUDE.md` is\n  classified as settings tamper (`settings_tamper_claude_md_excludes_root`).\n- The harness gate adds an advisory finding, `root_instruction_load`. It\n  reports `root_load_unobservable` unless you pass a complete list of the","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011M6LGziZQtx1AzmkibXuQx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
