{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0121ykhGMtk3KK15QajxWXR2","type":"tool_result","content":"82\tdef _session_roots_guard(\n83\t    candidate_paths: List[str],\n84\t    repo_root: Path,\n85\t    session_id: str,\n86\t    env=None,\n87\t) -> Optional[str]:\n88\t    \"\"\"Pure deny predicate for writes under session-registered roots.\n89\t\n90\t    Returns a block-reason string (→ deny) or ``None`` (→ allow). The DENY\n91\t    path is PURE in the ``_candidate_is_granted`` sense: no emission side\n92\t    effects — the deny audit breadcrumb is a once-per-event concern owned\n93\t    by ``main()``. The ONE carve-out is the corrupt / unreadable /\n94\t    unknown-schema registry branch: a PRESENT-but-unparseable registry is a\n95\t    security-matcher INPUT-parse failure (FXγ / C3), so it fails CLOSED for\n96\t    external writes (deny) while allowing repo-internal writes, and it\n97\t    additionally fires a best-effort tamper audit event (H5 observability,\n98\t    see below) so a registry-rewrite attack is both blocked and visible.\n99\t    ``env`` defaults to ``os.environ`` (injectable for tests).\n100\t\n101\t    Deny returns are VALUES, never raises: an unexpected raise out of this\n102\t    function is a hook bug (INFRA) and the ``main()`` call site fail-OPENs\n103\t    it with a breadcrumb, per the CLAUDE.md §4 split.\n104\t    \"\"\"\n105\t    src_env = env if env is not None else os.environ\n106\t\n107\t    # ---- Load registry (absent OR infra-read-error → silent allow;\n108\t    # ---- present-but-unparseable → fail-CLOSED for external writes) ----\n109\t    registry_path = _session_roots_registry_path(repo_root)\n110\t    try:\n111\t        if not registry_path.is_file():\n112\t            return None\n113\t    except OSError:\n114\t        return None\n115\t    # C3 (codex/grok R4+R5): read raw BYTES in a SEPARATE try — an OSError here\n116\t    # (PermissionError, IsADirectory, transient IO) is an INFRASTRUCTURE read\n117\t    # failure, NOT tampering, so it fails OPEN (allow), same as an ABSENT file.\n118\t    # Folding it into the parse `except Exception` below turned an infra read\n119\t    # error into a fail-CLOSED external-write DENY — a self-DoS. Reading BYTES\n120\t    # (not text) keeps the utf-8 DECODE inside the fail-closed parse block:\n121\t    # non-utf-8 bytes raise UnicodeDecodeError (IS-A ValueError, NOT OSError),\n122\t    # which is PRESENT-but-unparseable security-matcher input and MUST\n123\t    # fail-CLOSED — an earlier `read_text(encoding=...)` let that escape to the\n124\t    # caller's infra catch-all and wrongly ALLOW a binary registry overwrite\n125\t    # (R5). Only an OSError (bytes not readable at all) is INFRA -> allow.\n126\t    try:\n127\t        raw_bytes = registry_path.read_bytes()\n128\t    except OSError:\n129\t        return None\n130\t    try:\n131\t        data = json.loads(raw_bytes.decode(\"utf-8\"))\n132\t        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n133\t            raise ValueError(\"registry root is not an object\")\n134\t        if data.get(\"schema\") != _SESSION_ROOTS_SCHEMA:\n135\t            raise ValueError(\"unknown registry schema\")\n136\t        sessions = data.get(\"sessions\")\n137\t        if not isinstance(sessions, dict):\n138\t            raise ValueError(\"registry 'sessions' is not an object\")\n139\t    except Exception as exc:  # registry PRESENT + readable but unparseable\n140\t        print(\n141\t            \"[check_canonical_edit] session-roots registry unreadable \"\n142\t            f\"({type(exc).__name__}); present-but-unparseable security-matcher \"\n143\t            \"input → fail-CLOSED for external writes; repo-internal writes stay \"\n144\t            f\"governed by the canonical stack. registry={registry_path}\",\n145\t            file=sys.stderr,\n146\t        )\n147\t        # FXγ (C3) — DOCTRINE CORRECTION. A registry that EXISTS but cannot be\n148\t        # parsed (corrupt JSON, unknown schema-version, malformed `sessions`)\n149\t        # is PRESENT-but-unparseable security-matcher input, NOT an\n150\t        # ABSENT-file INFRA condition, so per CLAUDE.md §4 it fails CLOSED —\n151\t        # content the guard cannot parse is blocked, never waved through. The\n152\t        # deny is SCOPED to writes we cannot prove repo-internal (the new\n153\t        # surface an added root could have opened); repo-internal writes stay\n154\t        # governed by the canonical stack above (unaffected). Registry ABSENT\n155\t        # (handled earlier) remains an allow — the observer may simply not\n156\t        # have run.\n157\t        #\n158\t        # H5 observability is preserved and now UNCONDITIONAL: whether or not\n159\t        # this event carries an external write, the corruption is recorded in\n160\t        # the HMAC audit chain so a registry-rewrite attack leaves a forensic\n161\t        # trace. Emitted BEFORE the partition so even the allow (repo-internal"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"046a4659-3b58-4aa1-afb8-7e9a6b625253","uuid":"bc167d66-9637-4189-bfa6-2670ca055f05","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:08:14.545Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/canonical_edit/session_roots.py","content":"def _session_roots_guard(\n    candidate_paths: List[str],\n    repo_root: Path,\n    session_id: str,\n    env=None,\n) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Pure deny predicate for writes under session-registered roots.\n\n    Returns a block-reason string (→ deny) or ``None`` (→ allow). The DENY\n    path is PURE in the ``_candidate_is_granted`` sense: no emission side\n    effects — the deny audit breadcrumb is a once-per-event concern owned\n    by ``main()``. The ONE carve-out is the corrupt / unreadable /\n    unknown-schema registry branch: a PRESENT-but-unparseable registry is a\n    security-matcher INPUT-parse failure (FXγ / C3), so it fails CLOSED for\n    external writes (deny) while allowing repo-internal writes, and it\n    additionally fires a best-effort tamper audit event (H5 observability,\n    see below) so a registry-rewrite attack is both blocked and visible.\n    ``env`` defaults to ``os.environ`` (injectable for tests).\n\n    Deny returns are VALUES, never raises: an unexpected raise out of this\n    function is a hook bug (INFRA) and the ``main()`` call site fail-OPENs\n    it with a breadcrumb, per the CLAUDE.md §4 split.\n    \"\"\"\n    src_env = env if env is not None else os.environ\n\n    # ---- Load registry (absent OR infra-read-error → silent allow;\n    # ---- present-but-unparseable → fail-CLOSED for external writes) ----\n    registry_path = _session_roots_registry_path(repo_root)\n    try:\n        if not registry_path.is_file():\n            return None\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    # C3 (codex/grok R4+R5): read raw BYTES in a SEPARATE try — an OSError here\n    # (PermissionError, IsADirectory, transient IO) is an INFRASTRUCTURE read\n    # failure, NOT tampering, so it fails OPEN (allow), same as an ABSENT file.\n    # Folding it into the parse `except Exception` below turned an infra read\n    # error into a fail-CLOSED external-write DENY — a self-DoS. Reading BYTES\n    # (not text) keeps the utf-8 DECODE inside the fail-closed parse block:\n    # non-utf-8 bytes raise UnicodeDecodeError (IS-A ValueError, NOT OSError),\n    # which is PRESENT-but-unparseable security-matcher input and MUST\n    # fail-CLOSED — an earlier `read_text(encoding=...)` let that escape to the\n    # caller's infra catch-all and wrongly ALLOW a binary registry overwrite\n    # (R5). Only an OSError (bytes not readable at all) is INFRA -> allow.\n    try:\n        raw_bytes = registry_path.read_bytes()\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    try:\n        data = json.loads(raw_bytes.decode(\"utf-8\"))\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"registry root is not an object\")\n        if data.get(\"schema\") != _SESSION_ROOTS_SCHEMA:\n            raise ValueError(\"unknown registry schema\")\n        sessions = data.get(\"sessions\")\n        if not isinstance(sessions, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"registry 'sessions' is not an object\")\n    except Exception as exc:  # registry PRESENT + readable but unparseable\n        print(\n            \"[check_canonical_edit] session-roots registry unreadable \"\n            f\"({type(exc).__name__}); present-but-unparseable security-matcher \"\n            \"input → fail-CLOSED for external writes; repo-internal writes stay \"\n            f\"governed by the canonical stack. registry={registry_path}\",\n            file=sys.stderr,\n        )\n        # FXγ (C3) — DOCTRINE CORRECTION. A registry that EXISTS but cannot be\n        # parsed (corrupt JSON, unknown schema-version, malformed `sessions`)\n        # is PRESENT-but-unparseable security-matcher input, NOT an\n        # ABSENT-file INFRA condition, so per CLAUDE.md §4 it fails CLOSED —\n        # content the guard cannot parse is blocked, never waved through. The\n        # deny is SCOPED to writes we cannot prove repo-internal (the new\n        # surface an added root could have opened); repo-internal writes stay\n        # governed by the canonical stack above (unaffected). Registry ABSENT\n        # (handled earlier) remains an allow — the observer may simply not\n        # have run.\n        #\n        # H5 observability is preserved and now UNCONDITIONAL: whether or not\n        # this event carries an external write, the corruption is recorded in\n        # the HMAC audit chain so a registry-rewrite attack leaves a forensic\n        # trace. Emitted BEFORE the partition so even the allow (repo-internal","numLines":80,"startLine":82,"totalLines":364}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0121ykhGMtk3KK15QajxWXR2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
